{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/separator-vs-motif-check.md", "content": "# Full-width separator versus salient interior motif

When a test grid has a complete horizontal/vertical line alongside a conspicuous patterned region, don't select a transform just because the motif is visually rich. Start from exact input/output diffs in every demo. Record separator coordinates and whether separator cells change, plus every changed isolated marker and unchanged lookalikes. Compare candidate rules that operate on the separator/bands against rules operating on the motif, and require a single candidate to explain all positive and negative cells before projecting to the test.

In this instance, a minimal demo changed a sparse marker and recolored the adjacent full-width row below a full-width divider; another recolored the row immediately above a full-width divider while preserving a lone marker. The test had a bottom full-width color-2 row and an unrelated dense color-1 patch. The initial guess recolored the row above the divider and copied the patch as if it were a template; it was rejected. Do not infer a color-8 band merely by mirroring the first demo's local row offset. Need more discriminating evidence to establish which band relative to divider is selected and what sparse markers indicate.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u007/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint/references/separator-vs-motif-check.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-error-recovery", "file": "references/multi-rejection-demo-discipline.md", "content": "# Multi-rejection discipline for demo-driven ARC

In a task with one or more incorrect submissions, an incorrect result falsifies the whole proposed generator. Do not resubmit a near-identical grid or make an evidence-free palette/coordinate tweak. Rebuild a ledger from every input-output pair, including unchanged cells, and separately test scope, geometry, mapping, and palette.

When multiple candidate grids have failed and the remaining evidence does not determine the test output, stop guessing. State internally at least two concrete hypotheses and the specific observation that distinguishes them. Request one demo only if it can supply that observation, then integrate it before the next action; never treat receiving a demo as an automatic reason for another request. Submit only when one candidate explains every pair and unambiguously yields the test grid. Keep each turn to one action-only JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u007/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-error-recovery/references/multi-rejection-demo-discipline.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint-update", "file": "references/sparse-line-gap-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Sparse collinear gap inference

When demos contain separated runs or sparse markers of one color, compare exact changes along both axes. Record maximal same-color runs and isolated markers by row/column, then test whether output fills only cells between collinear same-color endpoints. Determine endpoint eligibility and scope independently: identical-looking gaps may remain unchanged in an identity or partial-transformation demo. A broad “fill every gap” rule is not supported unless it reproduces every changed and unchanged cell across the cumulative demo ledger.

After any rejected prediction, discard the complete fill generator and revisit trigger, orientation, and target selection; do not merely remove a few gaps. This is a candidate mechanism to test against the full examples, not a universal ARC rule.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u007/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-checkpoint-update/references/sparse-line-gap-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-integration-discipline", "file": "references/curator-review-lesson.md", "content": "# Curator review lesson: make the demo checkpoint operational

A repeated failure mode is issuing successive demo requests on heterogeneous Continual-ARC pairs without integrating each response, then making an unsupported test guess. A generic instruction to "identify competing hypotheses" is too easy to skip. Before any next request, the solver should have explicitly compared exact diffs (including identity/no-op and unchanged structures), stated the strongest cross-demo relation and unresolved alternatives, and named a concrete observation that a new example could distinguish. If that comparison cannot be made, stop requesting and synthesize the current evidence first.

Postmortem references should not become proxy evidence about the current task. Load one only to answer a specific identified question, then return to the actual demo grids and verify the rule cell-by-cell. A previous session's outcome is a cautionary workflow note, not proof of this task's transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/seen/play/users/u007/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-demo-integration-discipline/references/curator-review-lesson.md"}
